          "version": "1.3.0",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CODE-HARDCODED-SECRET",
              "name": "CodeHardcodedSecret",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Possible hard-coded secret in lib/sqlite/sqlite3.c"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "source_code",
                  "confirmed_misconfiguration"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "1 secret-like value(s) of type generic_assignment (first at line 106875). Values were redacted by QSI. Move secrets to a secrets manager/environment; rotate the exposed values. Heuristic pattern match on source text without data-flow analysis. The usage context (security-relevant or not) must be confirmed manually; treat as preliminary."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1 secret-like value(s) of type generic_assignment (first at line 106875). Values were redacted by QSI. Move secrets to a secrets manager/environment; rotate the exposed values. Heuristic pattern match on source text without data-flow analysis. The usage context (security-relevant or not) must be confirmed manually; treat as preliminary."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://owasp.org/www-project-application-security-verification-standard/"
            },
            {
              "id": "RNG-WEAK-KEYGEN",
              "name": "RngWeakKeygen",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Non-cryptographic generator next to key or seed generation in src/crypto/SecretKey.h"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "source_code",
                  "potential_crypto_risk"
                ],
                "security-severity": "7.5"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "'random()' at line 59, within a few lines of 'SecretKey' (1 occurrence(s)). Use the platform CSPRNG (secrets, os.urandom, crypto.getRandomValues, crypto/rand, SecureRandom, OsRng) or a hardware TRNG for every key, seed and mnemonic. Heuristic pattern match on source text without data-flow analysis. The usage context (security-relevant or not) must be confirmed manually; treat as preliminary."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "'random()' at line 59, within a few lines of 'SecretKey' (1 occurrence(s)). Use the platform CSPRNG (secrets, os.urandom, crypto.getRandomValues, crypto/rand, SecureRandom, OsRng) or a hardware TRNG for every key, seed and mnemonic. Heuristic pattern match on source text without data-flow analysis. The usage context (security-relevant or not) must be confirmed manually; treat as preliminary."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "DEP-KNOWN-ADVISORY",
              "name": "DepKnownAdvisory",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "stellar-xdr 21.2.0 is affected by GHSA-x57h-xx53-v53w (CVE-2026-29795)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "dependencies",
                  "known_vulnerable_component"
                ],
                "security-severity": "4.0"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "OSV.dev reports GHSA-x57h-xx53-v53w (CVE-2026-29795) for crates.io package stellar-xdr at version 21.2.0: stellar-xdr's StringM::from_str bypasses max length validation. The advisory names no affected functions, so reachability was not determined. Upgrade to a fixed version (25.0.1). Advisory data comes from OSV.dev at the recorded time. Reachability is judged from names, not a call graph: the affected functions the advisory lists against the identifiers in the audited product code, a Cargo feature it says is off by default against the audited manifests, the platforms it lists against the declared Rust targets. Indirect calls (traits, macros, other dependencies) are not followed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "OSV.dev reports GHSA-x57h-xx53-v53w (CVE-2026-29795) for crates.io package stellar-xdr at version 21.2.0: stellar-xdr's StringM::from_str bypasses max length validation. The advisory names no affected functions, so reachability was not determined. Upgrade to a fixed version (25.0.1). Advisory data comes from OSV.dev at the recorded time. Reachability is judged from names, not a call graph: the affected functions the advisory lists against the identifiers in the audited product code, a Cargo feature it says is off by default against the audited manifests, the platforms it lists against the declared Rust targets. Indirect calls (traits, macros, other dependencies) are not followed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-x57h-xx53-v53w"
            },
            {
              "id": "CODE-QV-ASYMMETRIC",
              "name": "CodeQvAsymmetric",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Quantum-vulnerable X25519: key wrapping and data encryption at rest (1 file(s))"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "post_quantum",
                  "pq_long_term_risk"
                ],
                "security-severity": "5.0"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "1 reference(s) to X25519 across 1 file(s), e.g. lib/libsodium/src/libsodium/crypto_box/crypto_box_seal.c:32. Role, judged from paths, function names and nearby code: key wrapping and data encryption at rest. Keys or data encrypted to these public keys are stored: anyone who copies them now decrypts them once a CRQC exists (harvest now, decrypt later). Wrap keys with ML-KEM or a hybrid KEM (X25519 + ML-KEM-768) and re-wrap the stored keys; data that may already have been copied needs its own assessment. This is a forward-looking risk that depends on the arrival of a cryptographically relevant quantum computer (CRQC). No such computer is publicly known to exist today; the timeline is uncertain. It is not a present-day exploitable vulnerability. Heuristic pattern match on source text without data-flow analysis. The usage context (security-relevant or not) must be confirmed manually; treat as preliminary."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "1 reference(s) to X25519 across 1 file(s), e.g. lib/libsodium/src/libsodium/crypto_box/crypto_box_seal.c:32. Role, judged from paths, function names and nearby code: key wrapping and data encryption at rest. Keys or data encrypted to these public keys are stored: anyone who copies them now decrypts them once a CRQC exists (harvest now, decrypt later). Wrap keys with ML-KEM or a hybrid KEM (X25519 + ML-KEM-768) and re-wrap the stored keys; data that may already have been copied needs its own assessment. This is a forward-looking risk that depends on the arrival of a cryptographically relevant quantum computer (CRQC). No such computer is publicly known to exist today; the timeline is uncertain. It is not a present-day exploitable vulnerability. Heuristic pattern match on source text without data-flow analysis. The usage context (security-relevant or not) must be confirmed manually; treat as preliminary."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://doi.org/10.1137/S0097539795293172"
            },
